diff --git a/TODO.md b/TODO.md
index 0f0aa20..7a3d0d5 100644
--- a/TODO.md
+++ b/TODO.md
@@ -8,3 +8,19 @@
 - `schema.sql:19` — the comment maps `bank_transaction_code.description` to the ISO 20022 domain. Check this against the Enable Banking `BankTransactionCode` schema. If `description` is free text, the `tx_code` column and its comment are both wrong.
 - `seb.py` `Config.from_env` — `EB_ASPSP` lets the user pick another bank, but `aspsp_country` is fixed at "LT" and the module doc and the schema comment assume SEB. Either drop `EB_ASPSP`, or add `EB_ASPSP_COUNTRY` and make the docs bank-neutral.
 - `schema.sql` — an existing `dengi.db` keeps its old table, so it lacks the new generated `is_debit` column. Nothing reads that column yet. Add a migration (for example `ALTER TABLE txn ADD COLUMN` guarded by `PRAGMA table_info`) before any query relies on it.
+
+## Unsmell run 2026-11-01, second pass (seb.py, schema.sql, pyproject.toml, .gitignore)
+
+The schema items below change generated columns of `txn`. Because `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` never changes an existing table, ship them together with the migration planned above, so that an old `dengi.db` and a new one do not end up with different column definitions.
+
+- `schema.sql:20` — `concat_ws` skips NULL arguments. A transaction with no sub-family therefore gets a two-part code that reads as domain/family, and one with no `bank_transaction_code` gets `''`. Make `tx_code` NULL unless all three parts are present, or store the three parts as separate columns.
+- `schema.sql:10` — `is_debit` is NULL when `credit_debit_indicator` is missing, and then `amount_cents`, `counterparty` and `counterparty_iban` treat the row as a credit. Either add `CHECK (raw ->> '$.credit_debit_indicator' IN ('DBIT', 'CRDT'))`, or reject such a transaction in `Transaction.parse` in `seb.py`.
+- `schema.sql:12` — a non-numeric `transaction_amount.amount` becomes `amount_cents = 0` without any error. Add a CHECK on the amount, or validate it in `Transaction.parse`.
+- `schema.sql:15-16` — the rule "the creditor on a debit, the debtor on a credit" is written twice. A generated `counterparty_side` column (`IIF(is_debit, 'creditor', 'debtor')`), used in both JSON paths, would state the rule once. `counterparty` holds only a name, so rename it to `counterparty_name` in the same migration.
+- `schema.sql:27` — the index name `txn_date` says neither which date it covers nor that it is per account. `txn_account_booking_date` would. An existing db keeps the old index, so drop it as part of the migration.
+- `pyproject.toml:19` and `seb.py` `Store.__init__` — the schema file's name and its place next to the module are spelled in both files. Renaming either breaks only an installed `seb`. The wheel also installs a bare `schema.sql` at the top level of site-packages. Options: move the code into a `dengi/` package and load the schema with `importlib.resources`, or embed the schema in `seb.py` as a string. Either one changes how the program is laid out and run.
+- `.gitignore:3-5` and `seb.py` `DB`, `Session.PATH`, `Config.from_env` (`private.pem`) — every runtime file sits in the current directory, and `.gitignore` has to repeat each name. A new state file can be committed by accident, and these files hold bank data, a consent and a private key. Keep the runtime state in one directory, for example the XDG state directory, and ignore that directory.
+- `seb.py` `Session.parse` — a `valid_until` without a timezone parses, and the comparison with `datetime.now(UTC)` in `Session.load` then raises a TypeError instead of a BankError. Reject a naive `valid_until` in `Session.parse`.
+- `seb.py` `user_ip` and `EnableBanking.call` — a network failure, a failed ipify call, or a body that is not JSON raises a `requests` exception or a `ValueError`. Neither is a BankError, so the run ends in a traceback. Decide whether these should become BankError.
+- `seb.py` `Transaction.booked` and `Account.parse` — `entry_reference`, and an account's `uid` and `iban`, are typed `str` but never checked. A null `uid` ends up in the transactions URL and in `txn.iban`. A null or numeric value gets through and fails later, in `Store.upsert_counting_new`, with an sqlite IntegrityError or a non-text key. Check their types where they are parsed.
+- `seb.py` `Transaction.parse` and `Store.last_booking_date` — `booking_date` is first parsed when a later run resumes. A malformed value then raises ValueError on every run after it. Parse the date when the transaction is read, or add a `CHECK (date(booking_date) IS booking_date)` to the schema together with the migration.
